I'm Lynn George I'm the CEO of Sun run  changing the future of energy here in  the US Sun one's business model is to  offer basically solar as a service to  consumers so consumers like solar energy  they get it but they think it's  expensive they think it's a lot of work  to you know figure out exactly what kind  of technology they want to put on the  rooftop so we took away all those  problems by going to a homeowner and  saying we'll pay to install the system  on your roof you just essentially have  to lend us your roof and then we'll just  bill you each month for your electricity  just like your utility company but it's  actually cheaper and it's green and it's  produced on your rooftop  back in business school of my classmate  at Fenster approached me and said I have  an idea we just looked at the market and  it was just so obviously huge I mean  there are very few opportunities to  enter a market where even in its  earliest phase you know you can undercut  six billion dollars in annual sales and  so you just felt like it intrinsically  made sense and you know is the sort of  the classic you know Business School  Gees if I could just get 1% of this pie  you know this works and so you just had  conviction around that it wasn't one of  these scenarios where we spent a ton of  time doing research or writing business  plans we just said let's go try to bid  on a project we started while we were at  Stanford the company was run for seven  months you know half of the Attic of my  house in San Francisco and you know we'd  be up in the roof of my house you know  on cellphones trying to get these deals  to happen and we bid and I think it was  British Telecom wanted to build a big  system on one of their campuses in the  US and we went out and we pitched it we  said we can raise the capital behind  this let's give it a try and we  certainly didn't win that deal but it  made us realize that there was a market  and that we had a business and we  quickly pivoted over to the consumer end  market as opposed to the commercial on  market and that was a wise choice  we had no idea a lot of the ups and  downs along the way how painful it would  be how many sleepless nights we would  have it was a frightening ride for the  first couple years you know obviously as  we were just starting to close our first  project finance transaction over the  summer of 2008 you know the whole world  melted down you know we ended up closing  our first project finance fund the day  of the SP an eleven year loan in  November of 2008 and so when the markets  collapsed you know we were sitting there  faced with can we get this capital  raised do we have to shut the business  down it did also happen that you know in  the end of 2008 the law is subsidizing  solar all changed we had to restart  business and I remember ed and I looked  at each other and we just said we're  gonna get it done because we have to we  have no choice and so it's just you just  look straight forward you don't look at  that you don't turn to the side and you  just go for it in January 2009  you know we had 9 cents we have four  digits of monthly new origination  now we have raised the capital to  purchase about two billion dollars in  residential solar facilities about 1.6  billion of which we've purchased and  that growth is almost hard to sort of  comprehend because the business isn't  that much different it's just the number  of zeros kind of in the columns keep  increasing oftentimes when you go to  business school it's so easy to take a  safe career you know it's easy to get a  great consulting job or investing job or  investment banking job and and  oftentimes a lot of these you know smart  MBAs want to poke holes in businesses  and I think Stanford is unique in that  it really teaches you the analytical  skills to assess a business but without  losing that hope without losing that  optimism  that sort of resilience that's sort of  not giving up works and you know it's  common among many entrepreneurs when I  talk to my colleagues and you know  almost everybody has had those moments  where it feels as though there's no way  to succeed but what's common about the  people who do is that they don't give up  don't give up that's the secret  [Music]  you
 

